WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.920
Team Melisia leaves the Ocean Race Atlantic after recovering from a technical issue, while a leading trio pulls ahead of the rest of the fleet in rough conditions. This is Offshore North, Episode 4. One Canadian skipper is working to become the first from his country to finish the Vendée Globe alone, and without stopping. On the Canadian boat, the position of Emira Four stays unconfirmed after his departure from Collingwood in early August. The team's most recent public update, posted on the 2nd of September, summarized the summer training program

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:39.860
on Georgian Bay. His news section carried nothing after the 17th of August, and its YouTube channel last posted on the 31st of August with footage from the well-ent Canal Transit.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:58.080
That leaves the boat's current location and routing after the Great Lakes leg, dependent on whatever the next update supplies. The boat itself carries a clear pedigree into the campaign's 2028 target. It won the Ocean Race in 2023 as 11th hour racing team Malama under Charlie and Wright.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:12.080
Sebastian Simon then took it to 3rd place in the 2024 to 2025 Vendée Globe despite a broken foil, and Canada Ocean Racing acquired the current generation foiling in Mocha in early 2025.

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:27.560
The standing facts confirm it exceeds 18 meters in length and can exceed 40 knots. Every solo mile sailed on this platform now banks both experience and potential globe series points toward the qualification requirement of one grade two solo race finished on the boat,

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:41.240
within twice the winter's time. The next concrete step on that path remains the route Duran starting the 1st of November. Out in the fleet, the Ocean Race Atlantic sits at its midpoint after five days of a 3000 nautical mile course.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:57.260
Paul Mailhat on United by the Ocean, Francesca Clapsich on Team Francesca Clapsich powered by 11th hour racing, and Cogiro Shiraiishi on DMG Mori Global 1 have opened a gap of more than 430 miles on the remaining three Imokas.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:11.080
Sailworld reported that the leaders crossed ahead of a low pressure system that brought gusts above 30 knots and heavy slamming seas. Boris Herman's team Malaysia dropped back after an earlier technical problem yet still holds the overall lead.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:22.420
The same system forced repeated sail changes and reefing while crews struttle to move, eat or rest on board. Mailhat noted the fleet had reached halfway and hoped for steadier conditions ahead.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:36.540
With the boats now spread across hundreds of miles, small routing decisions carry larger weight for the finish into lorion. Embrace the challenge suffered a 3 meter tear in its main sail while reefing in 40 knot winds during the same low pressure system.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:51.160
Sailworld Europe stated the crew completed repairs at sea without stopping and returned the boat to full speed. The incident shows how a single equipment failure in heavy weather can erase hours or days of progress even when the hull itself stays intact.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:03:07.220
Sixteen solo sailors started the Golden Globe race from Lassabla to Lawn on the 6th of September. Sailors reported the addition follows the original 1968 to 1969 rules with no satellite navigation, no weather routing and no outside support.

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:16.400
Skippers must rely on sextant and paper charts for the entire nonstop unassisted circumnavigation. The start marks the third running of the revived event.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:26.200
Four ultim trimorans and 11 ocean 50 boats raced in the 24 hour ultim. The last major off-shore test before the route do rum begins on the 1st of November.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:37.720
While Evoie noted both fleets use the short format event to refine setups and crew coordination under race conditions. Results feed directly into route do rum preparation for the boats involved.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:49.020
That brings us to plain sailing. The physical demands on crews in a transatlantic race like the ocean race Atlantic come from constant motion and sleep disruption rather than any single storm.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:04:00.080
When a low pressure system passes, winds can jump from 15 knots to over 30 knots within hours. At forces repeated sail changes and reefing while the boat slams through short steep waves.

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:09.380
On a 60 foot Emocha, the motion is violent enough that simply moving from the cockpit to the galley or trying to prepare a freeze-dried meal becomes a full body effort.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:21.860
Crews often report going 24 hours or more without a proper hot meal or more than a few minutes of continuous rest. The six boat fleet in this addition met exactly those conditions on days 4 and 5.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.580
Sleep comes in short fragments because the boat must keep moving at high speed through the night.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:36.780
Even with an autopilot holding course, someone must alert for sudden wind shifts or equipment strain. And the noise of water slamming against the hull makes deeper rest difficult.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:48.220
Over five days these effects compound. Reaction time slow, small errors in sail trim or routing add up and the mental load of deciding whether to push harder or conserve the boat grows heavier.

00:04:48.220 --> 00:05:00.040
The leading trio of male hat, clapsich and shirashi use the low pressure system as a turning point. They sailed a route that placed them ahead of the worst weather while the rest of the fleet remained inside it.

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:07.160
Their decision to accept a slightly longer route for clearer air and better rest shows how these cumulative stresses shape strategy.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:14.360
The gap of more than 430 miles that opened after five days is the direct result of those choices made under fatigue.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:21.920
In ocean racing, the boat that manages its crew's energy most effectively often gains the largest advantage once the weather settles.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:30.700
Before we go, the countdown stands. 797 days remain until the Vande Globe start on the 12th of November, 2028.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:38.360
The campaign's next concrete milestone is the Root Duram, which starts on the 1st of November, 2026 from Saint Molo.

